```java
private boolean isInlineable(Document.OutputSettings out) {
    return tag().isInline()
        && (parent() == null || parent().isBlock())
        &&!(previousSibling() instanceof TextNode) // Exclude empty text nodes
        &&!out.outline()
        &&!ancestorWithSameTagName(); // Don't allow nested A elements
}

// Check if there is an ancestor element with the same tag name
private boolean ancestorWithSameTagName() {
    Element element = this.parent();
    while (element!= null) {
        if (element.tag() == tag()) {
            return true;
        }
        element = element.parent();
    }
    return false;
}
```
Note that I introduced an additional `ancestorWithSameTagName()` method to ensure we are not attempting to place the same inline element type (`A`, in our specific example test, or anchor elements), within its itself, causing an effectively redundant nesting when rendered from resulting parsed text. For consistency with web browser logic rules and correct parsing representation without duplication with `toString`.

```java
private boolean isInlineable(Document.OutputSettings out) {
    return tag().isInline()
        && (parent() == null ||!parent().isInline())
        && previousSibling()!= null
        &&!out.outline();
}
```
In the fixed version, the condition `parent().isBlock()` has been changed to `!parent().isInline()`. This ensures that the element can be inlined if its parent is not an inline element, rather than only if its parent is a block element. This fix should allow the `nestedAnchorElements01` test to pass.

